1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,282
Hello! If you live in Korea, you'll hear the question '어디에 있어요? ' really often.

2
00:00:07,282 --> 00:00:15,410
Today, we will learn expressions for describing locations. Then you can confidently guide others around our neighborhood.

3
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:26,258
Locations are usually spoken in the order of '장소 + 위치말 + 에'. For example, like '학교 앞에'.

4
00:00:26,258 --> 00:00:31,240
Today, let's learn location words slowly, one by one.

5
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:41,091
'앞' means the front side of something. For example, '학교 앞에서 만나요.

6
00:00:41,091 --> 00:00:51,560
' is said. You just attach '앞에' after a place. Now, repeat after me slowly. '앞'.

7
00:00:52,010 --> 00:01:02,444
'뒤' means the opposite of the front, the side you turn your back to. For example, '집 뒤에 공원이 있어요.

8
00:01:02,444 --> 00:01:08,620
' is said. Now, repeat after me slowly. '뒤'.

9
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:18,897
'옆' means right next to, the side side-by-side. For example, '은행 옆에 카페가 있어요.

10
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:27,290
' is said. Use it when two places are next to each other. Now, repeat after me. '옆'.

11
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:35,893
'위' means the higher side. For example, '책상 위에 책이 있어요.

12
00:01:35,893 --> 00:01:45,210
' is said. Use it to say what is on a surface. Now, repeat after me. '위'.

13
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:57,902
'밑' means the lower side, below. For example, '의자 밑에 가방이 있어요.

14
00:01:57,902 --> 00:02:09,080
' is said. '밑' and '아래' are similar. Now, repeat after me. '밑'.

15
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:19,582
'안' means the inside or interior of a certain space. For example, '가방 안에 책이 있어요.

16
00:02:19,582 --> 00:02:28,350
' is said. Use it to speak of the inside of something. Now, repeat after me. '안'.

17
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:42,087
'밖' is the opposite of '안', meaning the outside. For example, '집 밖에 자동차가 있어요.

18
00:02:42,087 --> 00:02:54,820
' is said. Remember '안' and '밖' as a pair. Now, repeat after me. '밖'.

19
00:02:55,270 --> 00:03:04,590
'사이' means the middle of two things. For example, '은행과 카페 사이에 있어요.

20
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:13,100
' is said. It points to the middle of two places. Now, repeat after me. '사이'.

21
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:23,521
'왼쪽' means the left side. For example, we say '왼쪽에 학교가 있어요. '.

22
00:03:23,521 --> 00:03:31,000
It is used very often when pointing to a direction. Now, repeat after me. '왼쪽'.

23
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:41,042
'오른쪽' means the right side. For example, '오른쪽에 마트가 있어요.

24
00:03:41,042 --> 00:03:52,740
' is said. Remember '왼쪽' and '오른쪽' together. Now, repeat after me. '오른쪽'.

25
00:03:53,190 --> 00:04:01,220
Now let's learn place names too. '마트' means a big store that sells goods.

26
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:09,250
We say '마트에서 우유를 사요. '. Now, repeat after me. '마트'.

27
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:19,837
'학교' means a place to study. We say '학교에 걸어서 가요. '.

28
00:04:19,837 --> 00:04:28,130
The '에' after a place shows direction. Now, repeat after me. '학교'.

29
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:37,664
'은행' and '카페' and '공원' also appear often. '은행 옆에 카페가 있어요.

30
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:45,640
' '공원 안에서 산책해요. ' You can use them with location words like this.

31
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:57,004
One more small rule. Use '에' for location and '에서' for action. '학교에 가요.

32
00:04:57,004 --> 00:05:05,070
' is direction, '학교에서 공부해요. ' is action there.

33
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:11,270
Now for a real situation. If someone asks '마트가 어디에 있어요?

34
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:19,320
', you answer '학교 앞에 있어요. '. Let's practice together.

35
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:27,060
One more time. '은행이 어디에 있어요? ' Then you can reply '카페 옆에 있어요.

36
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:33,790
' or '공원과 카페 사이에 있어요. '.

37
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:40,885
Let's also try giving directions. '왼쪽으로 가세요. 그리고 학교 앞에서 오른쪽이에요.

38
00:05:40,885 --> 00:05:46,720
'. If you connect location words, you can give directions. You're doing great!

39
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:59,770
Here are the location words we learned today. Front, back, beside, on, under, inside, outside, between, left, right.

40
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:11,835
Shall we read them aloud one more time? Wonderful! Be sure to use today's expressions.

41
00:06:11,835 --> 00:06:15,320
See you next time!
